\chapter{The Operators Feature: Predicate
Composition}\label{ch7-the-operators-feature-predicate-composition}

\section{Motivation}\label{ch7-motivation}

A predicate is a callable that maps a value to a Boolean. In isolation,
predicates are trivial --- a lambda, a function pointer, a small
functor. The difficulty appears when the rules a program must enforce
are combinations of many such checks: a value must be positive
\emph{and} even, or it must satisfy one of several alternative shape
requirements, or it must fail a check in order to be rejected. Writing
each composite rule by hand produces repetitive, error-prone lambdas
that are difficult to read and difficult to test in isolation.

The Operators feature provides a uniform wrapper,
\texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}Predicate\textless{}\allowbreak{}F\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}},
that turns any callable into a value with proper Boolean algebra. The
three overloaded operators \texttt{\&}, \texttt{|}, and \texttt{!} build
new predicates from existing ones, so a complex rule can be expressed as
an expression of named sub-rules. The result is closer to a small
specification language embedded in C++ than to a chain of nested
\texttt{if} statements.

This chapter describes the wrapper, its three operators, the
\texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}predicate} factory, and the
\texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}Operators} feature tag that
exposes the same machinery through a DSL-qualified
\texttt{make\_\allowbreak{}pred} factory. It covers short-circuit
semantics, value semantics, \texttt{constexpr} evaluability, operator
precedence, and a series of worked validators. Chapter 5 introduced
feature tags and the mixin mechanism; Chapter 6 covered the Pipeline
feature, which is frequently combined with Operators in the same DSL
definition.

\section{The Predicate Wrapper}\label{ch7-the-predicate-wrapper}

The central type of the feature is
\texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}Predicate\textless{}\allowbreak{}F\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}},
a thin struct parameterized on the callable type \texttt{F}. It holds a
single member, \texttt{fn}, of type \texttt{F}, and exposes the three
composition operators. The wrapper does not own any additional state,
perform any allocation, or impose any policy on what \texttt{F} may
capture. Its only contract is that \texttt{F} is callable with the value
passed to \texttt{operator(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}} and that the
result is convertible to \texttt{bool}.

The constructor is \texttt{explicit} and \texttt{constexpr}. Being
explicit prevents accidental implicit construction from an arbitrary
callable, which is important because the operators would otherwise be
selected against bare lambdas in surprising contexts. Being
\texttt{constexpr} allows a predicate to be constructed in a
constant-expression context, which becomes significant when composing
rules at compile time. The member \texttt{fn} is move-constructed from
the argument, so even stateful functors are transferred cheaply.

The call operator is a template on the argument type, taking a
forwarding reference \texttt{T\&\& v}. It forwards \texttt{v} into the
stored callable and returns the Boolean result. Because the call
operator is itself \texttt{const} and \texttt{constexpr}, a
\texttt{Predicate} is an immutable function object that can be evaluated
in a constant expression. The forwarding preserves value category where
the underlying callable allows it, which matters for move-only or
non-copyable argument types.

\begin{code}
template <typename F> struct Predicate
{
  F fn;
  explicit constexpr Predicate (F f) : fn (std::move (f)) {}

  template <typename T>
  constexpr bool
  operator() (T &&v) const
  {
    return fn (std::forward<T> (v));
  }
  // ... operators below ...
};
\end{code}

The wrapper is, in effect, a tiny function-object adapter. Its purpose
is not to add behaviour to \texttt{F} at construction time but to attach
the three composition operators to a value whose type would not
otherwise support them. A bare lambda has no \texttt{operator\&} that
means Boolean conjunction --- that operator would instead perform a
bitwise AND of the lambda's address with another value, which is
meaningless. By wrapping the lambda in
\texttt{Predicate\textless{}\allowbreak{}F\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}},
the overloaded operators become available.

\section{The predicate Factory}\label{ch7-the-predicate-factory}

Constructing a
\texttt{Predicate\textless{}\allowbreak{}F\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
directly requires spelling out \texttt{F}, which for a lambda is an
unspellable closure type. The library therefore provides a factory,
\texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}predicate}, that deduces
\texttt{F} from its argument. The factory is the only sanctioned way to
obtain a \texttt{Predicate} in user code; the constructor is technically
accessible but impractical for lambdas.

The factory's signature is a perfect-forwarding template. The stored
type is
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}decay\_\allowbreak{}t\textless{}\allowbreak{}F\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}},
which strips references, cv-qualifiers, and array-to-pointer decay so
that the member \texttt{fn} is a plain object type. This is the same
convention used throughout the standard library for type-erased or
wrapped callables. The argument is forwarded into the constructor, so a
named functor can be moved in and a temporary lambda can be moved or
copied as appropriate.

\begin{code}
template <typename F>
constexpr auto
predicate (F &&f)
{
  return Predicate<std::decay_t<F>>{ std::forward<F> (f) };
}
\end{code}

Because the factory returns \texttt{auto}, the result type is
\texttt{Predicate\textless{}\allowbreak{}std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}decay\_\allowbreak{}t\textless{}\allowbreak{}F\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}},
which is a concrete, named type that the compiler can print in
diagnostics even though the user never writes it. In practice a
predicate is almost always held in an \texttt{auto} variable, and the
concrete type is irrelevant to the user. The factory is
\texttt{constexpr}, so it can be used in a constant expression.

A predicate constructed from a function pointer works identically to one
constructed from a lambda. The decayed type of a function name passed to
the factory is a pointer-to-function, and
\texttt{Predicate\textless{}\allowbreak{}bool(\allowbreak{}*)\allowbreak{}(\allowbreak{}const int\&)\allowbreak{}\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
is a perfectly valid instantiation. This matters when a rule reuses an
existing free function rather than a freshly written lambda.

\section{Conjunction with operator\&}\label{ch7-conjunction-with-operator}

The \texttt{operator\&} overload implements conjunction: the composite
predicate reports \texttt{true} only when both operands report
\texttt{true}. The overload is a template on the right-hand operand's
callable type \texttt{G}, taking a
\texttt{Predicate\textless{}\allowbreak{}G\textgreater{}\allowbreak{} other}
by value. The body constructs and returns a new \texttt{Predicate} whose
stored lambda captures both callables, \texttt{a = fn} and
\texttt{b = other.\allowbreak{}fn}, by copy.

\begin{code}
/// Conjunction: both predicates must pass.
template <typename G>
constexpr auto
operator& (Predicate<G> other) const
{
  return Predicate{ [a = fn, b = other.fn] (auto &&v)
                      { return a (v) && b (v); } };
}
\end{code}

The lambda uses the built-in \texttt{\&\&} operator, which
short-circuits: if \texttt{a(\allowbreak{}v)\allowbreak{}} is
\texttt{false}, \texttt{b(\allowbreak{}v)\allowbreak{}} is not
evaluated. This is the only behaviour that makes sense for a validator,
because it avoids invoking the second predicate when the first has
already settled the outcome. A predicate with side effects --- say, one
that logs a message on a failing value --- would not be invoked past the
point where the result is determined.

The returned \texttt{Predicate} is a fresh, self-contained value. It
captures copies of the two underlying callables, so the operands can be
destroyed or reassigned without affecting the composite. There is no
shared state, no reference back to the originals, and no allocation on
the heap. The composite is the same kind of value as its operands: a
small struct holding one or more captured callables.

Because \texttt{operator\&} is \texttt{const}-qualified, conjunction can
be applied to a predicate held in a \texttt{const} variable. This is
consistent with the value-semantics model: a predicate is an immutable
rule, and combining two rules does not modify either of them. The result
is a new rule that can itself be combined further.

\section{Disjunction with
operator\textbar{}}\label{ch7-disjunction-with-operator}

The \texttt{operator|} overload implements disjunction. Its structure
mirrors \texttt{operator\&} exactly: a template on the right-hand
operand's callable type, a by-value parameter, and a returned
\texttt{Predicate} whose lambda captures both callables. The only
difference is the body, which uses \texttt{||} instead of \texttt{\&\&}.

\begin{code}
/// Disjunction: at least one predicate must pass.
template <typename G>
constexpr auto
operator| (Predicate<G> other) const
{
  return Predicate{ [a = fn, b = other.fn] (auto &&v)
                      { return a (v) || b (v); } };
}
\end{code}

The \texttt{||} operator short-circuits on \texttt{true}: once
\texttt{a(\allowbreak{}v)\allowbreak{}} returns \texttt{true},
\texttt{b(\allowbreak{}v)\allowbreak{}} is not evaluated. For a
disjunctive validator this means the first sub-rule that accepts the
value settles the result, and any subsequent predicates are skipped. As
with conjunction, this avoids unnecessary work and avoids side effects
in the skipped predicates.

Disjunction is the natural tool for expressing alternatives. A validator
that accepts any of several distinct value shapes --- a numeric constant
in one of several ranges, a string matching one of several prefixes ---
is most readable when written as
\texttt{rule\_\allowbreak{}a | rule\_\allowbreak{}b | rule\_\allowbreak{}c}.
Each alternative is named and testable in isolation, and the composite
is a single value that can be passed to a higher-level algorithm.

The value-semantics argument from conjunction applies unchanged. The
returned \texttt{Predicate} captures its operands by copy and has no
link back to them. The operands may be discarded immediately after the
expression, and the composite continues to behave correctly.

\section{Negation with operator!}\label{ch7-negation-with-operator}

The \texttt{operator!} overload is a unary prefix operator. It takes no
parameters and returns a new \texttt{Predicate} whose lambda captures
\texttt{a = fn} and returns \texttt{!a(\allowbreak{}v)\allowbreak{}}.
Negation does not short-circuit because there is nothing to
short-circuit; it is a single call to the underlying predicate followed
by a Boolean not.

\begin{code}
/// Negation.
constexpr auto
operator!() const
{
  return Predicate{ [a = fn] (auto &&v) { return !a (v); } };
}
\end{code}

Negation is most useful as a readability device. A rule that rejects a
particular shape can be written either as a positive predicate (``is not
in this set'') or as the negation of a positive predicate (``is in this
set, then negate''). The two are semantically equivalent, but the latter
often corresponds more directly to the way the rule is described in a
specification. Naming the positive predicate and then negating it keeps
the implementation aligned with the prose.

Because negation returns a \texttt{Predicate}, the result can be
combined further. \texttt{!pred \& other} negates \texttt{pred} and
conjoins the result with \texttt{other}. The operands of the inner
negation are evaluated first because \texttt{!} binds more tightly than
\texttt{\&}, which is the topic of the section on precedence below.

\section{The Operators Feature Tag}\label{ch7-the-operators-feature-tag}

The feature tag \texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}Operators} is the
bridge between the standalone
\texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}predicate} factory and the
DSL-qualified \texttt{make\_\allowbreak{}pred} factory described in
Chapter 5. The tag is an empty struct containing a nested \texttt{Mixin}
template. The mixin provides a single static member function,
\texttt{make\_\allowbreak{}pred}, that forwards its argument to
\texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}predicate}.

\begin{code}
struct Operators
{
  template <typename Derived> struct Mixin
  {
    template <typename F>
    static constexpr auto
    make_pred (F &&f)
    {
      return dsl::predicate (std::forward<F> (f));
    }
  };
};
\end{code}

The mixin's \texttt{make\_\allowbreak{}pred} is \texttt{static} and
\texttt{constexpr}, matching the factory it delegates to. A DSL that
lists \texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}Operators} in its feature
list gains
\texttt{MyDSL:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}make\_\allowbreak{}pred(\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}}
as a qualified name for constructing predicates. This is purely a naming
convenience: the returned object is exactly the same
\texttt{Predicate\textless{}\allowbreak{}std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}decay\_\allowbreak{}t\textless{}\allowbreak{}F\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
that \texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}predicate} would produce.
There is no per-DSL specialization, no additional behaviour, and no
dependency on the \texttt{Derived} type parameter.

The reason for providing the qualified name at all is consistency with
the rest of the toolkit. Other features --- Pipeline, Match, Rewrite ---
expose their factories through the DSL's own scope, and Operators
follows the same convention so that user code can be uniform across
features. A reader who encounters
\texttt{BasicDSL:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}make\_\allowbreak{}pred} in
a source file can locate the feature tag in the DSL declaration and
understand immediately where the name comes from.

The \texttt{Derived} template parameter is unused inside the mixin. It
is present because the CRTP base described in Chapter 3 instantiates
every feature mixin with the derived DSL type, and the parameter must
exist in the signature even when the mixin does not consume it. This
keeps the feature-tag protocol uniform across all features.

\section{A First Composition}\label{ch7-a-first-composition}

The basic-usage example from the distribution combines Pipeline and
Operators in a single DSL. A value is threaded through two pipes, and
two predicates are constructed and evaluated separately. The example
illustrates that the two features coexist without interference: a DSL
may list both in its feature list and use the factories of each.

\begin{code}
#include "MuDSL.hpp"
#include <iostream>

struct BasicDSL : dsl::DSL<BasicDSL, dsl::Pipeline, dsl::Operators> {};

int main() {
  auto v = BasicDSL::wrap(5) | dsl::pipe([](int x) { return x + 3; })
           | dsl::pipe([](int x) { return x * 2; });
  auto gt5 = BasicDSL::make_pred([](int x) { return x > 5; });
  auto even = BasicDSL::make_pred([](int x) { return x % 2 == 0; });
  std::cout << v << " " << (gt5(v) && even(v)) << "\n";
}
\end{code}

This example evaluates the two predicates separately and combines their
results with the built-in \texttt{\&\&} operator. That is a legitimate
use, but it does not exercise the Operators feature's composition
machinery. The next section shows how the two predicates can be combined
into a single composite rule before any value is tested.

\section{Combining Predicates into a
Rule}\label{ch7-combining-predicates-into-a-rule}

The real power of the Operators feature is that composition happens
before evaluation. Two predicates can be joined into a single value that
is itself a predicate, and that composite can be passed around, stored,
and applied to many values without re-stating the rule.

\begin{code}
#include "MuDSL.hpp"
#include <iostream>

struct BasicDSL : dsl::DSL<BasicDSL, dsl::Operators> {};

int main() {
  auto gt5   = BasicDSL::make_pred([](int x) { return x > 5; });
  auto even  = BasicDSL::make_pred([](int x) { return x % 2 == 0; });
  auto rule  = gt5 & even;

  std::cout << rule(16) << "\n";  // 1: >5 and even
  std::cout << rule(4)  << "\n";  // 0: not >5
  std::cout << rule(7)  << "\n";  // 0: odd
}
\end{code}

The variable \texttt{rule} holds a \texttt{Predicate} whose closure type
contains copies of both lambdas. Each call to \texttt{rule} evaluates
the first lambda and, if it returns \texttt{true}, evaluates the second.
The composition is built once and reused for every input; there is no
per-call allocation or re-construction.

This pattern scales. A rule may be the conjunction of three, four, or a
dozen sub-rules, and the resulting composite is still a single
\texttt{Predicate} value. The sub-rules can be named individually, which
makes the rule readable and individually testable, while the composite
is the value that production code actually applies.

\section{A Numeric Range Validator}\label{ch7-a-numeric-range-validator}

A common use of predicate composition is range validation. A value is
acceptable when it lies within an inclusive lower and upper bound.
Expressed directly, the rule is the conjunction of a lower-bound
predicate and an upper-bound predicate.

\begin{code}
auto lo = dsl::predicate([](int x) { return x >= 0; });
auto hi = dsl::predicate([](int x) { return x <= 100; });
auto in_range = lo & hi;

in_range(50);   // true
in_range(-1);   // false, short-circuits at lo
in_range(101);  // false, lo passes, hi fails
\end{code}

Expressed this way, the rule mirrors the way a specification would
describe it: ``the value must be greater than or equal to zero, and less
than or equal to one hundred.'' Each bound is a named, independently
testable predicate. The composite is the conjunction of the two.

Because conjunction short-circuits, a value below the lower bound never
reaches the upper-bound check. For a pair of integer comparisons this is
a trivial saving, but the same structure applies to predicates whose
evaluation is expensive --- a database lookup, a regular-expression
match, a remote service call. Ordering the operands so that the cheaper
predicate is evaluated first is a simple and effective optimisation.

\section{A String Validator}\label{ch7-a-string-validator}

String validators benefit from composition because real-world string
rules are rarely single-condition. A username might need to be
non-empty, begin with a letter, and contain only alphanumeric
characters. Each of these is a separate predicate, and the composite is
their conjunction.

\begin{code}
#include "MuDSL.hpp"
#include <cctype>
#include <string>
#include <string_view>

auto non_empty = dsl::predicate([](std::string_view s) {
  return !s.empty();
});
auto starts_alpha = dsl::predicate([](std::string_view s) {
  return !s.empty() && std::isalpha(static_cast<unsigned char>(s[0]));
});
auto alnum_only = dsl::predicate([](std::string_view s) {
  for (char c : s)
    if (!std::isalnum(static_cast<unsigned char>(c))) return false;
  return true;
});

auto valid_username = non_empty & starts_alpha & alnum_only;
\end{code}

The composite \texttt{valid\_\allowbreak{}username} is a single
predicate that accepts only strings satisfying all three sub-rules.
Because \texttt{operator\&} is left-associative, the expression is
parsed as
\texttt{(\allowbreak{}non\_\allowbreak{}empty \& starts\_\allowbreak{}alpha)\allowbreak{} \& alnum\_\allowbreak{}only},
which is exactly the intended grouping. Short-circuiting means that an
empty string is rejected by the first operand and never inspected by the
later, more expensive checks.

This example also illustrates that the argument type does not have to be
\texttt{int}. The call operator is a template on the argument type, so
any type the underlying callable accepts can be passed. A predicate
declared against
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}string\_\allowbreak{}view}
accepts both \texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}string} and
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}string\_\allowbreak{}view}
arguments, and even string literals, through implicit conversion.

\section{Disjunction for Alternative
Shapes}\label{ch7-disjunction-for-alternative-shapes}

Disjunction is the natural tool when a value is acceptable if it matches
any one of several shapes. Consider a configuration value that may be
either a small integer constant or one of a fixed set of sentinel
strings. The two cases are most readable as separate predicates joined
by \texttt{|}.

\begin{code}
auto small_int = dsl::predicate([](int x) { return x >= 0 && x < 8; });
auto sentinel  = dsl::predicate([](std::string_view s) {
  return s == "auto" || s == "default" || s == "inherit";
});
\end{code}

Mixing argument types across the operands of a single composite is only
meaningful if the composite is applied to values that all operands can
accept. In practice, a configuration system that holds
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}variant\textless{}\allowbreak{}int,\allowbreak{} std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}string\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
values would write a small adapter predicate that dispatches on the
variant's index, and the disjunction would then be expressed inside that
adapter. The Operators feature does not impose any type unification; it
simply forwards the argument to each underlying callable and relies on
overloading or generic lambdas to handle the dispatch.

A more straightforward disjunction involves a single value type. A
port-number validator might accept the privileged range, the registered
range, or the dynamic range, with any of the three being acceptable.

\begin{code}
auto privileged = dsl::predicate([](int p) { return p >= 0 && p <= 1023; });
auto registered = dsl::predicate([](int p) { return p >= 1024 && p <= 49151; });
auto dynamic    = dsl::predicate([](int p) { return p >= 49152 && p <= 65535; });
auto valid_port = privileged | registered | dynamic;
\end{code}

Each branch is named and independently meaningful. The composite is a
single predicate that accepts any valid TCP or UDP port number.
Short-circuiting ensures that once a branch returns \texttt{true}, the
remaining branches are not evaluated.

\section{Negation in Practice}\label{ch7-negation-in-practice}

Negation is most often used to invert a predicate whose positive form is
the natural way to describe a condition. A rule that rejects values
equal to a sentinel can be written as the negation of an equality check.

\begin{code}
auto is_sentinel = dsl::predicate([](int x) { return x == -1; });
auto not_sentinel = !is_sentinel;
auto usable = not_sentinel & in_range;   // in_range defined above
\end{code}

The composite \texttt{usable} accepts values in range that are not the
sentinel. Writing the rule this way keeps the specification aligned with
the prose: ``the value must not be the sentinel, and must be in range.''
The alternative --- writing a single lambda that combines both
conditions --- would obscure the structure of the rule and would not
allow the sub-rules to be tested in isolation.

Negation also composes with disjunction. De Morgan's laws apply
directly: \texttt{!(\allowbreak{}a \& b)\allowbreak{}} is equivalent to
\texttt{!a | !b}, and \texttt{!(\allowbreak{}a | b)\allowbreak{}} is
equivalent to \texttt{!a \& !b}. The library does not perform any such
rewriting; it evaluates the expression as written. The choice of which
form to use is a matter of readability, not of efficiency, because both
forms produce the same observable short-circuit behaviour up to the
order of evaluation.

\section{Composing Three or More
Predicates}\label{ch7-composing-three-or-more-predicates}

Because each operator returns a fresh \texttt{Predicate}, there is no
fixed arity on composition. Three predicates can be conjoined with two
uses of \texttt{\&}, four with three, and so on. The resulting value is
a single \texttt{Predicate} whose closure contains copies of every
underlying callable, nested through the closure types of the
intermediate composites.

\begin{code}
auto positive = dsl::predicate([](int x) { return x > 0; });
auto even     = dsl::predicate([](int x) { return x % 2 == 0; });
auto small    = dsl::predicate([](int x) { return x < 100; });
auto good     = positive & even & small;
\end{code}

The expression \texttt{positive \& even \allowbreak{}\& small} is parsed
as \texttt{(\allowbreak{}positive \& even)\allowbreak{} \& small}
because \texttt{operator\&} is left-associative. The inner
\texttt{positive \& even} produces a \texttt{Predicate} whose closure
holds copies of the first two lambdas; the outer \texttt{\&} then
produces a \texttt{Predicate} whose closure holds a copy of that
intermediate and a copy of the third lambda. Evaluation walks the tree
in left-to-right order, short-circuiting at the first \texttt{false}.

The same structure applies to disjunction and to mixed compositions. The
depth of the resulting closure tree is proportional to the number of
operands, but in practice this is irrelevant: the tree is flattened by
the compiler's inliner, and a chain of conjunctions compiles down to a
sequence of calls with branches. There is no runtime cost to building
the composite, and the per-call cost is the cost of the underlying
callables plus a small number of branches.

\section{Mixed Conjunction, Disjunction, and
Negation}\label{ch7-mixed-conjunction-disjunction-and-negation}

Real rules often combine all three operators. A password-policy rule,
for example, might require that a candidate be at least twelve
characters long, contain at least one digit, and not be one of a small
set of well-known weak passwords.

\begin{code}
auto long_enough = dsl::predicate([](std::string_view s) {
  return s.size() >= 12;
});
auto has_digit = dsl::predicate([](std::string_view s) {
  for (char c : s) if (std::isdigit(static_cast<unsigned char>(c))) return true;
  return false;
});
auto is_common = dsl::predicate([](std::string_view s) {
  return s == "password123" || s == "letmein12345";
});
auto accept = long_enough & has_digit & !is_common;
\end{code}

The composite reads almost like the specification. Each sub-rule is
named, the conjunctions short-circuit, and the negation inverts the
common-password check. A reviewer reading this code can verify each
sub-rule against the policy document without untangling a single large
lambda.

Mixed compositions also arise in numeric validation. A value might need
to be positive and either even or divisible by five. Expressed directly,
this is a conjunction whose right-hand operand is a disjunction.

\begin{code}
auto divisible_by_5 = dsl::predicate([](int x) { return x % 5 == 0; });
auto rule = positive & (even | divisible_by_5);
\end{code}

The parentheses are load-bearing here, and their significance is the
topic of the next section.

\section{Operator Precedence and
Parentheses}\label{ch7-operator-precedence-and-parentheses}

C++ assigns \texttt{!} higher precedence than \texttt{\&}, and
\texttt{\&} higher precedence than \texttt{|}. These precedences are
inherited by the overloaded operators because overload resolution does
not change the parsing of expressions. An expression written without
parentheses is parsed according to the language rules, which may not
match the intended grouping.

The expression \texttt{a \& b | c} is parsed as
\texttt{(\allowbreak{}a \& b)\allowbreak{} | c}, not as
\texttt{a \& (\allowbreak{}b | c)\allowbreak{}}. If the intended rule is
``a holds, and either b or c holds,'' the parentheses are mandatory.
Omitting them silently produces a different rule that accepts any value
for which \texttt{c} holds, regardless of \texttt{a}.

The expression \texttt{!a \& b} is parsed as
\texttt{(\allowbreak{}!a)\allowbreak{} \& b}, which is usually what the
writer intends. The expression \texttt{!a | b} is parsed as
\texttt{(\allowbreak{}!a)\allowbreak{} | b}, again usually intended.
Even in these cases, explicit parentheses are recommended because they
make the grouping visible to a reader who may not have the precedence
table memorised.

A simple discipline eliminates the entire class of precedence bugs:
whenever two different operators appear in the same expression,
parenthesise every binary operand. Unary \texttt{!} applied to a single
named predicate does not require parentheses, but
\texttt{!(\allowbreak{}a \& b)\allowbreak{}} does, because the operand
of \texttt{!} is a binary expression. The library cannot enforce this
discipline; it is a coding-standard matter.

\begin{code}
// Intended: positive AND (even OR divisible by 5).
auto rule = positive & (even | divisible_by_5);

// Different rule, parsed as (positive & even) | divisible_by_5.
auto wrong = positive & even | divisible_by_5;
\end{code}

The second line above is not a bug in the library; it is the standard
C++ parsing of an unparenthesised expression. The Operators feature
overloads operators that already have precedences, and those precedences
are honoured by the compiler. The recommendation throughout this manual
is to parenthesise.

\section{Value Semantics and Capture}\label{ch7-value-semantics-and-capture}

Every composition produces a fresh \texttt{Predicate} that captures its
operands by copy. The composite has no reference back to the operands,
no shared mutable state, and no allocation. Once the expression has been
evaluated, the operands can go out of scope and the composite continues
to behave correctly.

This is a direct consequence of the way the operators are defined. Each
operator's lambda initialises two captures, \texttt{a = fn} and
\texttt{b = other.\allowbreak{}fn}, by copy. The captures are members of
the closure type, and the closure is in turn the member \texttt{fn} of
the new \texttt{Predicate}. The composite is a value that owns its
constituents.

Value semantics have two practical consequences. First, a composite
predicate can be returned from a function, stored in a container, or
moved across threads without any lifetime concerns. The composite is
self-contained. Second, the operands are not modified by composition. A
predicate can be used in multiple composites, and each composite holds
an independent copy of the underlying callable.

For stateful callables --- functors with non-\texttt{const} members,
lambdas that capture by reference --- the value-semantics model still
holds at the level of the \texttt{Predicate}, but the user must reason
about the lifetimes of the captured references. A lambda that captures a
local variable by reference and is wrapped in a \texttt{Predicate}
produces a predicate that dangles once the local goes out of scope. This
is the same rule that applies to any capturing lambda; the Operators
feature neither introduces nor eliminates the hazard.

\section{Constexpr Evaluation}\label{ch7-constexpr-evaluation}

Every operation in the feature is \texttt{constexpr}: the constructor,
the call operator, all three composition operators, and the
\texttt{predicate} factory. A predicate can be constructed, composed,
and evaluated entirely at compile time. This makes the feature suitable
for use in \texttt{static\_\allowbreak{}assert}, in template arguments,
and in any other context that requires a constant expression.

\begin{code}
constexpr auto is_positive = dsl::predicate([](int x) { return x > 0; });
constexpr auto is_even     = dsl::predicate([](int x) { return x % 2 == 0; });
constexpr auto rule        = is_positive & is_even;

static_assert(rule(4));
static_assert(!rule(-2));
static_assert(!rule(3));
\end{code}

The lambdas in this example are themselves constexpr, which is permitted
since C++17. The \texttt{Predicate} constructor and the
\texttt{operator\&} body are constexpr, so the composite \texttt{rule}
is a constant expression. The \texttt{static\_\allowbreak{}assert} lines
evaluate the composite at compile time and fail the build if the rule
does not hold.

Compile-time evaluation is useful for encoding invariants that must hold
for a program to be correct. A configuration table whose entries must
satisfy a complex rule can be validated at compile time, so that a
violation is a build error rather than a runtime failure. Chapter 18
discusses memoization, which can be combined with compile-time
predicates to build lookup tables; Chapter 19 discusses lazy values,
which can defer the evaluation of a predicate until its result is
actually needed.

\section{An Even-and-Positive Rule}\label{ch7-an-even-and-positive-rule}

A frequent teaching example is the rule ``the value is positive and
even.'' The Operators feature expresses this rule directly from its
prose description.

\begin{code}
auto is_positive = dsl::predicate([](int x) { return x > 0; });
auto is_even     = dsl::predicate([](int x) { return x % 2 == 0; });
auto rule        = is_positive & is_even;

rule(4);   // true
rule(3);   // false, odd
rule(-2);  // false, negative
\end{code}

This is the example used in the header's own documentation. It is
deliberately small because the point is to show the structure of the
feature, not to solve a realistic problem. The same structure scales to
the larger validators shown earlier in this chapter.

The short-circuit behaviour is visible in the second and third calls.
For input \texttt{3}, \texttt{is\_\allowbreak{}positive} returns
\texttt{true} and \texttt{is\_\allowbreak{}even} returns \texttt{false};
the composite returns \texttt{false}. For input \texttt{-2},
\texttt{is\_\allowbreak{}positive} returns \texttt{false} and
\texttt{is\_\allowbreak{}even} is not evaluated; the composite returns
\texttt{false}. The observable result is the same in both cases, but the
path through the rule differs.

\section{Composing User-Defined
Predicates}\label{ch7-composing-user-defined-predicates}

The factory accepts any callable, including user-defined functors. A
functor with named members can be more readable than a lambda when the
rule is non-trivial, and it can be reused across several composites.

\begin{code}
struct in_closed_range {
  int lo, hi;
  constexpr bool operator()(int x) const { return x >= lo && x <= hi; }
};

auto adult_age   = dsl::predicate(in_closed_range{18, 120});
auto senior_age  = dsl::predicate(in_closed_range{65, 120});
auto either      = adult_age | senior_age;   // equivalent to adult_age here
auto neither     = !either;
\end{code}

A functor with state --- here, the bounds \texttt{lo} and \texttt{hi}
--- is captured by value into the \texttt{Predicate}, and the composite
captures the \texttt{Predicate} by value in turn. The bounds are part of
the rule, not part of the value being tested, so they belong in the
functor. This pattern is appropriate when a single functor type can
represent a family of related rules parameterised by a few integers.

Functors are also useful when a rule requires helper methods or carries
documentation. A lambda is anonymous and cannot carry comments attached
to its type, while a named struct can be documented as a unit. For a
rule that appears in a public API, the named struct is often preferable.

\section{A Password-Policy Rule}\label{ch7-a-password-policy-rule}

Combining the techniques above, a realistic password policy can be
expressed as a single composite predicate. The policy requires a minimum
length, an upper-case letter, a lower-case letter, a digit, and the
absence of a common weak password.

\begin{code}
auto long_enough = dsl::predicate([](std::string_view s) {
  return s.size() >= 12;
});
auto has_upper = dsl::predicate([](std::string_view s) {
  for (char c : s) if (std::isupper(static_cast<unsigned char>(c))) return true;
  return false;
});
auto has_lower = dsl::predicate([](std::string_view s) {
  for (char c : s) if (std::islower(static_cast<unsigned char>(c))) return true;
  return false;
});
auto has_digit = dsl::predicate([](std::string_view s) {
  for (char c : s) if (std::isdigit(static_cast<unsigned char>(c))) return true;
  return false;
});
auto is_common = dsl::predicate([](std::string_view s) {
  return s == "Password123!" || s == "Letmein12345";
});

auto policy = long_enough & has_upper & has_lower & has_digit & !is_common;
\end{code}

The composite is a single value that can be stored in a variable, passed
to a validation function, or applied to many candidates. Each sub-rule
is independently testable, which simplifies unit testing: a test for
\texttt{has\_\allowbreak{}digit} can pass strings with and without
digits without setting up the rest of the policy.

The order of the operands is significant for performance but not for
correctness. The cheapest checks --- length and the presence of a digit
--- appear first, so that an expensive check like
\texttt{is\_\allowbreak{}common} is only reached when the cheap checks
have already passed. For a string that is too short, none of the
character-class scans is performed. This kind of ordering is a minor
optimisation, but it is free: the conjunction short-circuits, so
reordering the operands costs nothing and may save work.

\section{Short-Circuit Semantics in
Detail}\label{ch7-short-circuit-semantics-in-detail}

The short-circuit behaviour of the composite predicates is a direct
consequence of using the built-in \texttt{\&\&} and \texttt{||}
operators inside the lambdas. The built-in operators guarantee that the
right-hand operand is not evaluated when the left-hand operand settles
the result. Because the lambdas invoke the captured callables as
ordinary function calls, the same guarantee applies to the predicates.

For conjunction, the right-hand callable is not invoked when the
left-hand callable returns \texttt{false}. For disjunction, the
right-hand callable is not invoked when the left-hand callable returns
\texttt{true}. Negation always invokes its single operand.

This behaviour matters in three situations. First, when the right-hand
predicate has side effects --- logging, instrumentation, mutation of a
captured counter --- those side effects are skipped when the left-hand
predicate settles the result. Second, when the right-hand predicate is
expensive --- a regular-expression match, a database lookup --- the cost
is avoided for inputs that the left-hand predicate already rejects.
Third, when the right-hand predicate has preconditions that the
left-hand predicate guarantees --- for example, indexing into a
container that the left-hand predicate has checked is non-empty --- the
short-circuit ensures that the precondition is met before the right-hand
predicate runs.

The library does not document or enforce any of these uses. It simply
inherits the short-circuit semantics of the built-in operators, and the
user may rely on them as they would rely on \texttt{\&\&} and
\texttt{||} in ordinary C++ code.

\section{The Difference from Bitwise
Operators}\label{ch7-the-difference-from-bitwise-operators}

The \texttt{\&}, \texttt{|}, and \texttt{!} tokens are overloaded for
\texttt{Predicate} to mean Boolean conjunction, disjunction, and
negation. In ordinary C++ for integral operands, \texttt{\&} and
\texttt{|} are bitwise operators that evaluate both operands and combine
their bit patterns. The overload set for \texttt{Predicate} does not
change the meaning of these operators for other types; it only provides
additional overloads that are selected when both operands of \texttt{\&}
or \texttt{|} are \texttt{Predicate} values, and when the operand of
\texttt{!} is a \texttt{Predicate}.

A common mistake is to combine a \texttt{Predicate} with a raw callable
using \texttt{\&}. The expression \texttt{pred \& lambda} does not
compile, because the right-hand operand is not a \texttt{Predicate} and
the overload is not selected. The raw callable must first be wrapped
with \texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}predicate} or
\texttt{make\_\allowbreak{}pred}. The \texttt{explicit} constructor
prevents an implicit conversion from the callable to a
\texttt{Predicate}, which would otherwise make the expression compile
but with surprising semantics.

Another common mistake is to combine a \texttt{Predicate} with a
\texttt{bool} using \texttt{\&}. The expression
\texttt{pred(\allowbreak{}x)\allowbreak{} \& true} performs a bitwise
AND of two \texttt{bool} values, which is well-defined but evaluates
both operands. This is rarely the intent; the user almost always means
\texttt{pred(\allowbreak{}x)\allowbreak{} \&\& true}, which uses the
built-in logical AND. The library cannot prevent this mistake because
\texttt{pred(\allowbreak{}x)\allowbreak{}} is a \texttt{bool}, not a
\texttt{Predicate}, and the overloaded operators do not apply.

\section{Capturing State in Lambdas}\label{ch7-capturing-state-in-lambdas}

A lambda that captures by value produces a closure with value members,
and the \texttt{Predicate} holds that closure by value. This is the safe
default: the composite is self-contained and has no lifetime concerns. A
lambda that captures by reference produces a closure with reference
members, and the \texttt{Predicate} holds that closure by value --- but
the references inside the closure still refer to the original objects.

A predicate that captures a reference to a local variable and is
returned from the function in which it was created produces a dangling
reference. This is the same hazard that applies to any capturing lambda,
and the Operators feature neither introduces nor eliminates it. The user
must ensure that any object referred to by a captured reference outlives
the predicate.

For mutable state, a lambda declared \texttt{mutable} produces a closure
whose members can be modified by the call operator. Wrapping such a
lambda in a \texttt{Predicate} produces a predicate whose
\texttt{operator(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}} is \texttt{const}, which
means the call operator cannot modify the closure. The library's
\texttt{operator(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}} is
\texttt{const}-qualified, so a mutable lambda's modifications are not
observable through a \texttt{Predicate}. This is deliberate: a predicate
is an immutable rule, and side effects that mutate the rule's own state
would break value semantics. Users who need mutable state should hold
the state outside the predicate and capture it by reference, with the
lifetime considerations that entails.

\section{A Compile-Time Table
Validator}\label{ch7-a-compile-time-table-validator}

Combining \texttt{constexpr} evaluation with a small data structure, a
program can validate a table of constants at compile time. The build
fails if any entry violates the rule, which is a strong guarantee: the
program cannot be compiled with an invalid table.

\begin{code}
constexpr bool check_table() {
  auto positive = dsl::predicate([](int x) { return x > 0; });
  auto even     = dsl::predicate([](int x) { return x % 2 == 0; });
  auto rule     = positive & even;

  int table[] = {2, 4, 6, 8, 10};
  for (int x : table)
    if (!rule(x)) return false;
  return true;
}

static_assert(check_table());
\end{code}

This pattern is useful for embedded tables, lookup constants, and any
data that is known at compile time and must satisfy an invariant. The
predicate is constructed inside the \texttt{constexpr} function, applied
to each entry, and the result is checked by
\texttt{static\_\allowbreak{}assert}. A violation is a hard build error,
which is the strongest form of validation available in C++.

The same pattern can be used with \texttt{consteval} to force evaluation
at compile time even when the result is not used in a
constant-expression context. A \texttt{consteval} function that
constructs and applies a predicate is guaranteed to run entirely during
compilation, with no runtime overhead.

\section{Predicates in Pipelines}\label{ch7-predicates-in-pipelines}

Chapter 6 introduced the Pipeline feature, which threads values through
a chain of transforms. Predicates compose naturally with pipelines: a
value can be transformed and then tested, or a predicate can be used as
a gate that decides whether a transform is applied.

The two features are independent. A DSL may list both in its feature
list, as the basic-usage example shows, and the factories \texttt{pipe}
and \texttt{make\_\allowbreak{}pred} coexist without interference. A
predicate is not a pipe, and a pipe is not a predicate; the two are
different kinds of values that can be used together in the same program.

A typical combined use applies a pipeline to produce a value and then
applies a predicate to test the result. The predicate is constructed
once, outside the pipeline, and applied to the pipeline's output. This
separation keeps the transformation logic and the validation logic
distinct, which simplifies both.

\section{Predicates and the Rewrite
Feature}\label{ch7-predicates-and-the-rewrite-feature}

The Rewrite feature, covered in Chapter 13, uses predicates to decide
whether a rewrite rule applies to a given AST node. The predicates used
by Rewrite are ordinary callables; wrapping them in
\texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}Predicate} is not required by the
Rewrite machinery, but it is permitted. A wrapped predicate can be
composed with \texttt{\&}, \texttt{|}, and \texttt{!} before being
handed to a rewrite rule, which allows the rule's applicability
condition to be expressed as a combination of simpler checks.

This use is mentioned here only to point forward; the details of Rewrite
are the subject of Chapter 13 and Chapter 14. The relevant point for
this chapter is that the Operators feature produces ordinary function
objects, which can be consumed by any API that expects a callable taking
a node and returning a \texttt{bool}.

\section{Performance Considerations}\label{ch7-performance-considerations}

The Operators feature is designed to add no runtime overhead relative to
hand-written composition. Each operator constructs a \texttt{Predicate}
whose closure holds copies of the operands; the closure is inlined by
the compiler, and the resulting code is equivalent to a sequence of
calls with branches. There is no heap allocation, no virtual dispatch,
and no type erasure.

The call operator of the composite is a single call to the stored
lambda, which in turn calls the two captured callables and combines
their results with \texttt{\&\&} or \texttt{||}. The compiler inlines
the lambda body, inlines the captured callables, and produces a sequence
of tests with short-circuit branches. For simple callables --- integer
comparisons, character scans --- the generated code is indistinguishable
from a hand-written conditional.

The cost of constructing a composite is proportional to the number of
operands, because each operand is copied into the closure. For lambdas
with small captures, this cost is negligible. For functors with large
captures, the cost may be significant, and the composite should be
constructed once and reused rather than reconstructed for each input.
The value-semantics model encourages this pattern: a composite is a
value that can be stored in a variable and applied many times.

\section{Common Pitfalls}\label{ch7-common-pitfalls}

The most common pitfall is operator precedence. As discussed earlier, an
unparenthesised expression mixing \texttt{\&} and \texttt{|} is parsed
according to C++ rules, which may not match the intended grouping. The
remedy is to parenthesise every binary operand whenever two different
operators appear in the same expression.

A second pitfall is the difference between the overloaded operators and
the built-in bitwise operators. Combining a \texttt{Predicate} with a
non-\texttt{Predicate} using \texttt{\&} or \texttt{|} does not select
the overload and may produce a bitwise operation or a compile error. The
remedy is to wrap every callable in
\texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}predicate} before combining it.

A third pitfall is capturing by reference in lambdas that outlive the
captured objects. A predicate that captures a local by reference and is
returned from the enclosing function produces a dangling reference. The
remedy is to capture by value, or to ensure that the captured object
outlives the predicate.

A fourth pitfall is the \texttt{explicit} constructor. The expression
\texttt{pred \& [](\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}\{.\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}\}}
does not compile because the lambda is not implicitly convertible to a
\texttt{Predicate}. The remedy is to use the
\texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}predicate} factory, which
performs the explicit construction. The \texttt{explicit} qualifier is
deliberate, because implicit construction from arbitrary callables would
make the overload set surprising.

A fifth pitfall is assuming that \texttt{!} distributes over a
composite. The library does not rewrite
\texttt{!(\allowbreak{}a \& b)\allowbreak{}} into \texttt{!a | !b}; it
evaluates the expression as written, which means the inner conjunction
is evaluated first and then negated. The two forms are observationally
equivalent up to short-circuit order, but they are not the same value. A
user who wants the De Morgan form must write it explicitly.

\section{A Complete Example}\label{ch7-a-complete-example}

The following complete program exercises the feature end to end. It
defines a DSL with the Operators feature, constructs several predicates,
composes them into a rule, and applies the rule to a series of inputs.

\begin{code}
#include "MuDSL.hpp"
#include <cctype>
#include <iostream>
#include <string_view>

struct App : dsl::DSL<App, dsl::Operators> {};

int main() {
  using App::make_pred;

  auto positive = make_pred([](int x) { return x > 0; });
  auto even     = make_pred([](int x) { return x % 2 == 0; });
  auto small    = make_pred([](int x) { return x < 100; });

  auto good_int = positive & even & small;

  for (int x : {-2, 0, 3, 4, 50, 102, 64}) {
    std::cout << x << ": " << good_int(x) << "\n";
  }

  auto non_empty   = make_pred([](std::string_view s) { return !s.empty(); });
  auto starts_lt   = make_pred([](std::string_view s) {
    return !s.empty() && s[0] == 'L';
  });
  auto tag         = non_empty & starts_lt;

  for (std::string_view s : {"", "Hello", "Lib", "lab"}) {
    std::cout << s << ": " << tag(s) << "\n";
  }
}
\end{code}

The program defines two rules. The first accepts positive, even integers
below one hundred. The second accepts non-empty strings that begin with
the letter \texttt{L}. Each rule is a single value, applied to a series
of inputs. The output of the program is the Boolean result of each test,
printed one per line.

\section{Summary}\label{ch7-summary}

The Operators feature adds three overloaded operators to a small wrapper
type,
\texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}Predicate\textless{}\allowbreak{}F\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}},
turning any callable into a composable Boolean rule. Conjunction is
\texttt{operator\&}, disjunction is \texttt{operator|}, and negation is
\texttt{operator!}. Each operator returns a fresh \texttt{Predicate}
that captures its operands by value, so a composite is a self-contained
value with no shared mutable state and no allocation. Short-circuit
semantics are inherited from the built-in \texttt{\&\&} and \texttt{||}
operators, so a failing left-hand operand skips the right-hand operand's
evaluation.

The \texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}predicate} factory deduces
the callable type using
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}decay\_\allowbreak{}t}, so
lambdas and functors can be wrapped without spelling out their closure
types. The \texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}Operators} feature tag
exposes the same factory through a DSL-qualified
\texttt{make\_\allowbreak{}pred} static member function, following the
same mixin protocol used by the other features described in Chapter 5.
Both factories are \texttt{constexpr}, and every operation in the
feature is \texttt{constexpr}, so rules can be constructed and evaluated
at compile time for use in \texttt{static\_\allowbreak{}assert} and
other constant-expression contexts.

The chief practical advice is to parenthesise every binary operand
whenever two different operators appear in the same expression, because
the overloaded operators inherit the C++ precedences and an
unparenthesised expression may not group as intended. The other pitfalls
--- mixing \texttt{Predicate} with raw callables, capturing by reference
in long-lived predicates, and the \texttt{explicit} constructor --- all
reduce to the same principle: the feature provides a small, strict
wrapper, and the user is responsible for handing it wrapped values and
sensible lifetimes. With those caveats observed, the feature allows
complex validation rules to be expressed as readable expressions of
named sub-rules, with no runtime overhead relative to hand-written
conditionals.
